 ],
 "literal": "Did you see yourself? Or did you hear from those who saw? / Speak one thing clearly, as we desire to know! / Where white-tusked elephants bathe in the Sone river, / may you obtain gold-abounding Pataliputra! / From whose mouth did you hear of the coming of my lover?",
 "translation": "Did you see him yourself, or did you hear from those who saw him? Speak clearly so that we, who long for him, may know the truth. May you obtain the gold-abounding city of Pataliputra, where white-tusked elephants bathe in the Sone river! From whose mouth did you hear the news of my lover's return?",
 "modern": "நீயே அவரைப் பார்த்தாயா? அல்லது பார்த்தவர்களிடம் கேட்டாயா? நாங்கள் தெளிவுபெற விரும்புகிறோம், உண்மையைச் சொல்! வெண்மையான தந்தங்களை உடைய யானைகள் சோணை நதியில் நீராடும், பொன் நிறைந்த பாடலிபுத்திர நகரத்தையே நீ பரிசாகப் பெறுவாயாக! என் காதலர் வருவதைப் பற்றிய செய்தியை நீ யாருடைய வாயிலாகக் கேட்டாய்?",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her friend or a messenger who has brought the news of her lover's return. Filled with immense joy and disbelief, she wants to confirm if the news is absolutely true. She asks whether the informant saw him personally or heard it from others who did. In her gratitude and excitement, she blesses the informant to receive a reward as grand as the wealthy city of Pataliputra, where elephants bathe in the Sone river. This historical allusion to the wealth of Pataliputra highlights the immense value she places on the news of her lover's return.",
